window.UESCA_CONTENT['English/AppC-RunBAM.pdf'] = `<div class="content-body">
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:16px 0"><img src="images/AppC-RunBAM/p001_i01.png" alt="Figure page 1" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:4px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.1)"></figure>
<h2 class="section-head">Appendix C:</h2>
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:16px 0"><img src="images/AppC-RunBAM/p001_i02.png" alt="Figure page 1" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:4px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.1)"></figure>
<h2 class="section-head">Business &amp; Marketing</h2>
<figure style="text-align:center;margin:16px 0"><img src="images/AppC-RunBAM/p001_i03.png" alt="Figure page 1" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:4px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.1)"></figure>
<h2 class="section-head">Material In This Section Is Will Not Be Tested On</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Understanding how to market your serices is critical to your success as a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">running coach. The theory of “If You Build It, They Will Come” does not hold true</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">in regard to becoming a successful coach. Your success as a coach will be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">directly correlated to how good a coach you are and how effectiely you market</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">yourself and your serices.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Before you embark upon a marketing plan, you must know where you will focus</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">on marketing your serices. For example, an indiidual who is a coach at a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">health club will hae a ery different marketing strategy than someone who does</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">not work at a health club.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The next thing to determine when deciding upon a marketing strategy is the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">budget. If you are a coach who is solely marketing your serices “in-club,” there</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">might not be any costs incurred with marketing your serices. Additionally, there</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">are many ways to “guerrilla” market yourself and your coaching business that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">require little to no money. This is especially the case when marketed ia social</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">networking sites. Howeer, if you decide that you want to hae a marketing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">budget, you will need to determine what type of marketing will gie you the most</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">bang for your buck.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Traditional marketing aenues for coaches that typically require some sort of fee</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">are:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Eent exposition or sponsorship</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Magazine ads</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Internet banner ads</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Classified ads</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Mailing lists</h2>
<h2 class="section-head"> Website</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Assuming you will be looking for clients outside of a health club enironment, a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">website is a necessity. As most coaches do not hae their own physical location,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the website seres as the “store front” where prospectie and existing clients can</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">isit to learn about your serices, credentials, and areas of focus.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The critical factor to remember with your website design is that the quality and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">professional appearance of the website is a direct reflection on you as a coach.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Imagine what you would think if you walked by a storefront that was dilapidated</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and upon looking in the store, the lights were dim and the place was extremely</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">dirty. Needless to say, your first impression would be quite bad. The same holds</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">true for your website. It cannot be stressed enough that eery aspect of your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">business needs to be professional— including the website. When determining</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">what you want your website to look like, you should spend quite a bit of time</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">looking at other coaching sites and also at sites outside of the coaching realm.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Regardless of what site design you decide upon, it must be professional. This</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">means the following things:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Easy to naigate</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Clear and professional looking pictures and graphics</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• No spelling errors</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• All links/ideos and buttons must be functional</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• All information such as pricing and packages must be up to date</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• If you hae an online shopping cart it must be secure and fully functional</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• The site must be up and working. No matter how great your site is, if the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">serer is down or you hae technical issues that preent the site from</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">being iewed lie, this is a problem.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">One ery important aspect of a coaching website is testimonials. You will want to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">ask your clients with whom you hae had the most success if they would be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">willing to write a short testimonial for you to put on your website. Ideally, some or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">all of the testimonials should be located on the homepage. The purpose of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">testimonials is to alidate your effectieness and professionalism as a coach.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">As the website constitutes your storefront, you will get potential customers asking</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">questions ia email. You must answer these email inquiries within 24 hours—</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">without exception and, ideally, within 12 hours or less. The longer it takes to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">respond, the more it appears that you are not serious about your business or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">acquiring new clients.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">In regard to website deelopment, you should hire a professional who can design</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">a great-looking and functional website for you. Not only is a professional website</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">designer most likely going to do a better job than you, but they can also offer</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">guidance on issues like online shopping carts, search engine optimization, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the oerall structure of the site. Solely from a business and marketing standpoint,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">this is an area in which you will want to spend money, if you can. If you decide to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">try to create your own website, you hae seeral primary options. The first way is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to design it yourself using a “plug and play” site builder. These site builders</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">typically offer templates into which you can enter your logo, pictures, ideos, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">written content.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The next method is to build a website from scratch. Howeer, this takes a fair</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">amount of expertise in areas such as website editors, HTML coding, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Photoshop®. One of the most popular website building/editing tool is Adobe</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Dreamweaer®. This is a professional website deelopment tool and is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">considered by many to be the best website deelopment/editing software.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Website deelopers usually hae two primary pay structures. The first structure is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">a set price for a pre-determined number of website pages and additional charges</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">for additional pages/features and re-designs. The other structure is based on an</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">hourly fee. The most important thing from your end is to hae a good idea of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">what you think you would like the site to look like and what features and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">functionality you would want the site to hae—before contacting a deeloper.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This will not only decrease the cost of the site, but will most likely make the end</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">result exactly what you had in mind.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head"> Business Plan</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Many people think that the only reason to write a business plan is to attract</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">inestors. This is incorrect. Writing a business plan is a necessity for anyone</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">starting a business, no matter how large or small. The primary reason for writing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">a business plan is to create structure and to formalize your coaching process and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">ideas. The central part of a business plan is the mission statement. A mission</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">statement represents the primary focus of your business and the reason why it is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">so important is because anything and eerything you do within your business</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">should relate to the mission statement. A mission statement exists to ensure that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">you do not deiate from your original objectie and focus.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">A business plan typically has seeral areas. These areas are:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Executie summary</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Business description</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Market analysis including competition, competitie analysis, and growth</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">strategy</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Operations</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Management</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Financial components</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">While your business plan does not need to be long or inoled, it should address</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">most of the aboe areas. In regard to the financials, you probably will not need</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">in-depth financial reporting, howeer you will need to keep track of the basics.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">For example, you will need to know your starting cash position in regard to your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">budget and your estimated income and expenditures. While the financials can be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">reisited and reised as your business deelops, like the rest of the business</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">plan, the financials sere as a road map to keep you on track financially.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">For the purposes of this certification, the focus will be on the marketing aspect of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the business plan.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head"> Marketing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">While there are many ways to market your coaching business, it is adised to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">first look at low cost options before spending substantial amounts of money. For</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">starters, many of these low cost marketing options are often just, if not more</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">impactful than options that cost substantially more money.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The more marketing aenues you can utilize the better. The more ways a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">potential client hears and sees about your serice, the greater the chance they</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">will become a client.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Economical Marketing Methods</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Social Networking Sites</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Some of the most popular sites that a great for marketing a coaching business</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">®, Facebook®, LinkedIn®, YouTube®, Daily Mile®, and Instagram®.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">are: Twitter</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Posting on Message Boards and Serices Offered Sites</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Message boards are a great way to demonstrate your expertise in regard to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">contributing to a conersation thread. Just be sure to read the board’s terms of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">use, as many message boards do not allow solicitation or adertising.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Writing Articles for Magazines/Websites</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is a ery effectie way to get noticed as well as to adertise your expertise.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Be sure that your website and email are included at the end of the article.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Obiously, the more popular the magazine or website is, the more impact the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">article will hae.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Presentations/Lectures to Groups</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is perhaps the most effectie marketing aenue and is almost sure to result</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">in serious inquiries. The great thing about lectures is that you are face-to-face</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">with your potential clients. They can hear your expertise, experience your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">personality and most importantly, ask questions. Many triathlon, running, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">cycling clubs hae monthly or quarterly meetings. Additionally, many companies</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">hae a wellness component that puts on lectures from time to time about healthy</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">liing topics. All of these marketing aenues should be explored.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Newsletters</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">An important part of a website is a newsletter. This not only seres as a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">distribution list, but is also as a way to stay in contact with clients or potential</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">clients. You want to be sure to make the newsletter releant, which means</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">something that is a quick read and ery informatie at the same time. The goal is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to incent your newsletter subscribers to want to read the information rather than</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">sending it directly to the trash.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Distribution List</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">A distribution (or contact) list is your database of clients, potential clients, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">resources—essentially an electronic mailing list. The goal is to hae as large a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">distribution list as possible. The more people are on the list, the better the chance</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that you will get a new client. Many common uses of a distribution lists are</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">newsletters, emails, promotions, and general announcements. Be careful about</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">what and how often you contact your distribution list as you do not want to come</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">across as spam or irreleant. It is also adised that within your distribution list,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">you create categories such as clients, potential clients, businesses, friends, etc.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Partnering with Ancillary Businesses</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Relationships and partnerships with companies that offer serices and products</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">associated with running are inaluable. The alue of cross marketing serices,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">referrals, and promotions is immense. Remember, as a coach you are a single</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">person and to get as much exposure to your potential audience, you need the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">help of other established indiiduals and organizations. The goal in partnerships</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">is to create personal relationships with businesses. Therefore, once a solid</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">relationship is established, it would be wise not to seek out other like businesses</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to partner with. For example, if you partner with a running store to put on eents</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and cross promotions, you should not seek out other running stores to do the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">same thing, as it will likely dealue the relationship with the original running store.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">If you do plan to partner with competing stores in the same geographic area, you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">will need to let all of the businesses know your intentions up front.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">A partnership is not a one-way street—both parties should benefit from the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">relationship. A quick way to sour a business partnership is only to focus on what</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">you can get from the other side and not iew it as a mutually beneficial</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">relationship. Therefore, before approaching a business about a potential</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">partnership, be ready to explain your ideas on how you feel the partnership can</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">be beneficial to them.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Website Links</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Haing a section on your website that lists links to businesses with whom you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">partner is always a great way to adertise and proide additional resources to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your clients. Just make sure your business partners are doing the same for you.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Typically, there is no fee incurred, as it is purely an exchange.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Networking</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Networking is critical in the success of any business, but especially for one-</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">person business. While networking needs to be done tastefully, you should</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">always be attentie to how you can promote your business and serices. While</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">you can join professional networking groups, the most important step is to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">communicate as much as possible with as many people as possible. This is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">because successful networking is a numbers game. The more contacts you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">hae, the greater the chance one of those contacts will turn into or refer a client</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to you. A great place to network is at endurance sport expos and eents. You</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">might hae heard stories about how successful salespeople hae gained clients</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">while in the waiting room to see the dentist. You need to think and act along</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">these lines. You need to iew any situation as an opportunity to market yourself</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and your serices tactfully.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Volunteer Work</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Volunteering falls within the networking category. While olunteerism is important</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">regardless of the reason for doing so, it also presents a marketing opportunity.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is especially true if the charity is endurance sports related. Some endurance</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">sports-related olunteer work includes:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Coaching charity based teams</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Volunteering at eents</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Community-based exercise/fitness programs</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Referrals from Existing Clients and Health Professionals</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Your best referral source is your existing clients. If a client is happy with the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">serices they hae receied, they will typically be more than willing to refer you to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">others. You can een create a referral program that offers incenties to existing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">clients that refer other clients (e.g., a free month of coaching).</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">It is important to establish a network of professionals (i.e., physician,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">chiropractor, physical therapist, nutritionist, etc.) that you can refer to. This type</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">of relationship will bear fruit by becoming a two-way referral street. Additionally,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">by adertising this professional network on your website, you will most likely be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">iewed as much more credible than one that does not hae a network. A primary</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">reason for this is because one’s credibility is often authenticated by their</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">associations. In addition, by haing a network of referral sources, it shows that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">you take your coaching business seriously and understand that to obtain optimal</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">results, you’e adopted a team approach.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Blogging</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Blogs hae become a driing force in how companies and indiiduals market</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">themseles. The primary alue of blogs is that they are dynamic in nature ersus</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">most websites that are static. Due to this, online traffic and loyal readership are</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">often quite high with blogs that put up new content on a regular basis. Many large</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and established corporations hae identified the influence that bloggers hae in</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">regard to their loyal readership and thus look to partner with these blogs to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">promote their products and serices.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">It would be wise to consider either incorporating a blog on your website or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">making your website a blog. Regardless, the critical factor in haing a successful</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">blog is that it is updated frequently with fresh, releant content.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Write an eBook</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">A great way to demonstrate your expertise is to write an eBook. Typically,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">eBooks that are used for marketing purposes are often offered for free or for a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">nominal fee. Howeer, eBooks can also be used as a reenue source for your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">business. There are online shopping carts (e.g., www.e-junkie.com) that are</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">made specifically for selling eBooks.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Determining Effectiveness Of Marketing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">There are seeral ways to assess the effectieness of a marketing strategy.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Below are two of the more common and practical methods.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">1. Conersion Rate:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is represented by the number of people that sign up or purchase your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">serice out of the total number of people that you marketed to (e.g., flyers</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">mailed, number of people at seminar that you spoke at)</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">2. Cost to Acquire Client:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is determined by taking the total amount spend on adertising and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">diiding that by the number of clients. This can be done for the business</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">as a whole or based off of specific adertising campaigns. Obiously the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">lower the cost, the better.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Using these methods will greatly assist you in determining if your marketing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">strategy is profitable and to what degree.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Development Of Marketing Plan</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Successful marketing does not just happen, it requires planning. In order not to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">waste time, you need to ensure that your marketing strategies are effectie and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">align with your budget. Deelopment of a marketing plan can be broken up into</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">four steps:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">1. Determine Budget</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Your marketing budget will determine to some degree what strategies will be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">implemented. In regard to strategies that will incur a cost, you should prioritize</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">which ones are most important so that you are spending your money</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">intelligently. This step takes time especially if you are hiring someone for the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">web design. You will want to make sure you are getting the right person or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">company for the job and at the best possible rate.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">2. List and Categorize All Marketing Strategies</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Write out all potential marketing strategies regardless of how minor they may</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">seem.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">3. Implementation Dates and Schedule for Strategies</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Rank the strategies chronologically based on when they will be implemented.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Then assign the strategies implementation dates as well as a schedule for</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">each strategy (if the strategy is repeated). You will probably find that some</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">strategies will be repeated at set interals (e.g., newsletters) and others will</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">be on-going (e.g., coaching one-on-one with company logo on clothing). It is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">also helpful to use the strategy list to approximate which strategies will require</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the most work and time to implement. For example, website design and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">deelopment will require much more time than introducing yourself to the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">local running store manager.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">4. Dates of Re-Assessment</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Strategies are only useful if they work. Sometimes strategies do not delier a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">return on inestment (ROI) initially, but oer the long run they bear fruit.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Therefore, you want to gie your strategies enough time to work before</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">pulling the plug or re-tooling them. The best way to assess a strategy’s</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">effectieness is simply to ask those that inquire about your serices how they</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">heard about you. You need to record this data so that you can assess what</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the most effectie strategies are.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Where Are Your Clients?</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">How you market your serices is largely dependent in what capacity will you be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">working with them. The following areas are the primary settings.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Health Club</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Online</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Local/Regional</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Health Club</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">If you currently work or plan to work in a health club enironment, there are three</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">key demographics to whom you will need to be able to demonstrate expertise.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">These areas are your fitness manager or director, fellow staff members, and, of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">course, the club members.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The reason why your fitness manager is so critical is because they are the ones</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that often get requests for personal trainers and coaches. If your manager knows</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that you are a running coach and able to train clients for all three-sport</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">disciplines, you will be the first point of contact should someone ask for that type</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">of coach.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Referrals in health clubs come from all areas including other personal trainers</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and coaches, membership adisors, and een front desk receptionists.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Personal Trainers/Coaches: While on the surface, your fellow personal</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">trainers and coaches might seem like competition for clients, which in</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">some cases is true, it is up to you to distinguish yourself as a running</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">expert. One way to do this is to put on a running clinic for staff members.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">A clinic seres as a learning opportunity for the staff but also solidifies you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">as a running expert. This will increase the likelihood of getting referrals</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">from colleagues. Keep in mind that referrals are a two-way street and you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">need to refer clients to fellow colleagues as well.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Membership Adisors: They often sell not just memberships, but fitness</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">packages as well. Most gyms offer some type of complimentary fitness</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">session to new members and membership adisors will often inform the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">fitness director what the member’s goals are. If the adisor already knows</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that you are a running coach and a member is training for a running race,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">they will most likely recommend you.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Front Desk Receptionists: Receptionists are the first and last people that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">members see when they come in and leae. Because of this, many</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">members hae a great rapport with receptionists. Receptionists are the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">recipients of all sorts of questions including which fitness professional is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the best with whom to work.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">When marketing directly to members, there are seeral ways you can effectiely</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">market your expertise:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Make sure your staff bio clearly states you are a certified running coach. If</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your facility does not hae a staff bio board or some way to adertise the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">expertise of its staff, you need to push for this.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Hold a free run coaching workshop for members and make sure it is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">marketed well.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">o People like free stuff. Partner with a local sports store for free gie-</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">a-ways to entice people to come to the workshop.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Workout during busy times (if allowed) doing running-based exercises.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This is a great way to get members attention and is often a conersation</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">starter.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Work with the group fitness director to teach a running-based group</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">fitness class.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• If your health club offers specialized training packages, put together a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">running specific training package.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Start a running club.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Market your running success. Assuming you hae or currently participate</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">in running, work with your club to help market your participation and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">success. One way to do this is a raffle where members who guess closest</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to your race finishing time, wins a prize. This accomplishes two objecties:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">first, it gets members attention that you are a runner and second, it shows</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">off that you are the real deal!</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Online Coaching</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">In the age of technology and the internet, online coaching has really taken off. A</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">key benefit to online coaching is that you can proide your coaching serices</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">from anywhere and to anyone. It is encouraged to do an internet search of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">endurance sports coaching companies to see how they organize their website,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">what rate(s) they charge, how the coaching structured, etc.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">For many coaches, the biggest drawback to online coaching is that they do not</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">hae face-to-face contact with their client and are limited to phone and email</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">correspondence. In regard to simply creating a weekly or monthly training</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">program, this lack of face-to-face contact is not a large issue. The primary issue</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">comes into play when you need to assess their biomechanical form and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">demonstrate strength training exercises. Using relatiely inexpensie technology,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your client can ideo their form and send you the file to reiew. Additionally,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">when demonstrating strength training form, the use of ideo conferencing such</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">as Skype® is extremely useful. While there is no substitute for one-on-one</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">coaching, technology makes it such that een without face-to-face contact, all</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">aspects of coaching can be accomplished.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Local and Regional Coaching</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">If your primary target are local runners, the first question to ask yourself is based</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">on your business plan projections: Are there are enough local athletes to sustain</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your business model? Regardless of the number of local athletes, it is also</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">adisable to hae an online coaching serice, as this will only sere to increase</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your business. While networking is a critical element in a successful coaching, it</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">is especially the case when dealing primarily with local athletes. Of the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">aforementioned marketing areas, the ones most associated with marketing to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">local athletes are:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Presentations/lectures to groups</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Partnerships with ancillary businesses</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Referrals from existing clients and health professionals</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Volunteer at eents</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Marketing Focal Points</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Market Success</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">When it comes to coaching, especially in sports like running where performance</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">gains/losses are based on time, potential clients are looking for someone that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">can help them become faster and more efficient. In other words, when it comes</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to assessing performance, most runners are ery quantitatie in nature. This is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">where marketing your clients’ successes come into play. While it is great to say</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that you hae ten clients that hae achieed personal records (PR) in marathon</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">distance eents, what is more aluable is how much time they took off from their</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">preious marathon times. Getting the approal from and marketing your client’s</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">results is powerful and hard-hitting data that will entice others who want similar</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">performance gains to work with you. The great thing about the sport of running is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that data is aailable all oer the place. Quantitatie examples of gains made</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">under your coaching superision that could be mentioned are:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Decreased run by 10 minutes</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• No longer hae lower back pain when running</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Decreased body fat percentage by 5% in one month</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Increased aerage pace 1 minute per mile</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">As noted preiously, testimonials are ery powerful as a marketing tool as they</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">demonstrate positie experiences of your existing and past clients to potential</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">clients. For the same reason why many people do not purchase products unless</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">reading</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">reiews,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">testimonials</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">proide</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">“reiews”</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">ability</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">professionalism as a coach.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">All Encompassing Resource</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The more complete a resource you can be for your clients, the more aluable you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">will be to them. While you may not be a professional bike mechanic, if you can</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">help your client fix their derailleur so that they do not hae to take their bike into</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the shop, that is a huge alue both in time and money. You can only be as much</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">help as your scope of knowledge will allow you to be, but that is precisely the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">point. The broader your scope of knowledge is pertaining to the sport of running</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and all things that go along with it, the more inaluable you will be to your clients.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">This also ties directly to haing a professional network. Being able to refer your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">client to a registered dietician, physical therapist, chiropractors, orthopedic</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">physician, etc. demonstrates that you hae a strong network of professionals that</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">hae gotten results for clients with similar goals.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Qualifications</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Identify what makes you qualified to be a running coach and make this the center</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">point of your marketing. If you are reading this, you obiously hae chosen to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">become a certified running coach, which is the most specific qualification. While</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">being a certified running coach is typically the number one thing that most</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">potential clients will be looking for, there are other aspects that potential clients</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">might look for as well. Some of these could be:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Current or former runner</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Duration of coaching experience</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Degree in exercise physiology or related degree</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Personal trainer certification</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Athletic performance history</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Testimonials and/or references from past and current clients</h2>
<h2 class="section-head"> Operations</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Oer Delier</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">If you say you are going to do something, do it exactly as promised and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">consistently. For example, if you tell a client you are going to call them at 5 p.m.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to discuss their program, call them at 5 p.m., not at 5:03 or 5:05. While this</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">sounds like common sense and not that big a deal, it is not common sense and it</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">is a huge deal! Below are areas that often separate top coaches and personal</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">trainers from those who are less successful:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Punctual</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Consistent in the quality of the sessions</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Always hae a positie attitude</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Go aboe and beyond what the client expected</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Return e-mails/calls in a timely manner</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Document thoroughly all workouts</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Pay attention to the details</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Pricing</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">While on the surface, pricing seems like it should be pretty easy and simple to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">establish, rest assured that it is not. There are large olume books, MBAs, and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">psychology courses dedicated just to the subject of pricing. So, how does one</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">decide what to charge? While you can charge whateer you think your serices</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">are worth, it is adised to perform due diligence by researching and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">benchmarking other coaching serices. There are many factors in regard to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">pricing that you will need to consider. Some of these are:</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Price of Local Market: Coaching serices in NYC would likely be higher</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">than in a small rural town.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Volume: Will you discount if a client purchases a large package of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">coaching or would you consider this “dealuing” your serices?</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Niche: Are you trying to be a high-end specialty serice or bargain</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">basement “deal of the century” type of serice? If you are looking to be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">either of these, your pricing should be substantially aboe and below the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">aerage coaching pricing, respectiely.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Serices: You will hae to look at what other coaching serices offer for</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">their prices. For example, if a competitor offers a weekly coaching</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">program, analysis of heart rate and one coach-initiated phone call for $350</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">per month, you will need to analyze what you offer in comparison and offer</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">a competitie price option. This is where you can stand apart. Let us say</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that you offer the same serices as the example aboe, but in addition you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">also offer two calls a month, and a monthly strength/flexibility training</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">program. You could charge $350 to be a alue-based serice or charge</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">more based on the extra serices.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Cancellation Policy</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Most fitness facilities hae a cancellation policy and so should you. It is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">absolutely imperatie that prior to starting with a new client, you express to them</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">what the policy is as well as any exceptions to the policy. That said, it is</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">encouraged that you create a cancellation policy with no exceptions. Of course</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">things will come up (e.g., sickness, work related issues, transportation issues, no</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">child care, etc.), howeer by adopting a policy with built-in exceptions, you are</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">running a business model with a ery slippery slope in regard to cancellations.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Not all clients will intentionally take adantage of exceptions to cancel, but some</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">will. Maybe your 6 a.m. client does not want to get out of bed in the morning and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">calls to cancel at the last minute saying they are sick. If you allow sickness as an</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">exception to the policy, you will lose out on that session’s reenue. The “no</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">exception” policy sounds tough, but, in fact, it is the best insurance to acquire</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and maintain a steady and consistent client base.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">When implementing this policy, you must be fair. If you hae a “no exception”</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">policy with a 12-hour cancellation policy, you MUST be held to the same</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">standards. Meaning, if you are sick or your car breaks down and must cancel</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">within 12 hours of a session, the client gets a complimentary session. Many</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">fitness facilities hae strict cancellation policies, howeer the policy is typically a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">one-way street in regard to the client. This is extremely frustrating to the client</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and can lead to dissatisfaction to the extent that a client stops training or being</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">coached at that facility.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">The last point about the cancellation policy is that it often seres the purpose to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">weed out “would be” habitual cancellation clients. While you are running a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">business and you need to generate reenue from the serices you proide, you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">also are in the business of getting your clients the results they are seeking. By</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">addressing the cancellation policy prior to starting to work with a client, you will</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">often dissuade those that would cancel a lot from working with you. Do not iew</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">this as a negatie that you lost a potential client, but rather as a positie that you</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">are maintaining the integrity of your coaching business. In relation to the word of</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">mouth section aboe, een if you retain clients that habitually cancel, these types</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">of clients typically do not refer other clients, as they are not seeing results</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">themseles. It is a marketing dead end.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Run a Business—Not a Hobby</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Whether or not you hae one or one hundred clients, you need to iew and</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">operate your coaching practice as a business and not a hobby. This will be a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">critical element to your success as a running coach. Those that run their</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">“business” as a hobby often fall short in most operational areas. For example,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">maybe coaching is a side business for an indiidual and as a result, they late</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">cancel on their clients a lot due to their primary job responsibilities. This will hurt</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">their coaching business. Another example is if the coach does not charge what</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">would be considered the “fair market rate” for the particular area they are in or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">does not collect the coaching fees on time. Both of these examples lower the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">alue of the coach and the perception of the coach from the client will likely be</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">that of someone who is unprofessional and doing it as a “side” business.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">Een if you coach on the side and hae another primary job, it should not take a</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">back seat to operational excellence. If you do not hae the time or energy to</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">coach “professionally “ on the side due to family or primary job responsibilities, it</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">is better not to coach at all until you can commit to clients. Clients pay money for</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">your serices and should to be respected with your time, commitment, energy,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and professionalism.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head"> Summary</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Your marketing strategy will differ greatly based on whether you are an</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">independent coach or you work within an organization such as a health</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">club.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• A professional website is important as this is your irtual “storefront.”</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">o Right or wrong, the quality of your website often reflects upon the</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">quality of your serices.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Deelopment of a business plan is not just for acquiring inestors or</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">capital. A business plan helps to identify your goals, direction, marketing,</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">and the oerall trajectory of your business.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Economical marketing strategies offer a cost-effectie and impactful way</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">to attract potential clients.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">o Social media is at the forefront of economical marketing that has</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">the potential to be highly impactful.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• If marketing within a health club enironment, personal trainers, front desk</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">staff and membership adisors are often great sources of referrals.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• By tracking the conersion rate and the cost to acquire a client, you can</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">determine the effectieness of a marketing strategy.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• Perform extensie due diligence when determining how to price your</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">serices.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• The cancellation policy must be a two-way street.</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">• If you charge money for your serices, it is a job—not a hobby. The</h2>
<h2 class="section-head">business operations must reflect a high leel of professionalism.</h2>
</div>`;

